import{_ as s,C as r,o,c as n,j as t,a as i,E as l,a6 as h}from"./chunks/framework.CCKCzdWs.js";const c="/screenshots/pr-comment.png",k=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Pull-request feedback","description":"Piwi posts a run's result on the pull request, new failures apart from pre-existing ones, and sets a commit status from the merge gate's verdict.","frontmatter":{"title":"Pull-request feedback","description":"Piwi posts a run's result on the pull request, new failures apart from pre-existing ones, and sets a commit status from the merge gate's verdict.","lang":"en-US","head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://piwitests.dev/features/pr-feedback.html"}],["meta",{"property":"og:url","content":"https://piwitests.dev/features/pr-feedback.html"}],["meta",{"property":"og:title","content":"Pull-request feedback | Piwi for Playwright"}],["meta",{"property":"og:description","content":"Piwi posts a run's result on the pull request, new failures apart from pre-existing ones, and sets a commit status from the merge gate's verdict."}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"features/pr-feedback.md","filePath":"features/pr-feedback.md","lastUpdated":1794039811000}`),u={name:"features/pr-feedback.md"};function d(m,e,p,g,f,w){const a=r("Needs");return o(),n("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=t("h1",{id:"pull-request-feedback",tabindex:"-1"},[i("Pull-request feedback "),t("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#pull-request-feedback","aria-label":'Permalink to "Pull-request feedback"'},"​")],-1)),l(a,{reporter:"",scm:""}),e[1]||(e[1]=h('<p>The run URL a CI job prints is a link somebody has to click. Piwi can instead post the result onto the pull request itself, which is where the person who broke the test already is. To re-run a fixed cluster&#39;s tests from the dashboard, see <a href="./ci-rerun.html">Re-run in CI</a>.</p><h2 id="what-gets-posted" tabindex="-1">What gets posted <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-gets-posted" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What gets posted&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A comment and a commit status get posted when a run finishes, unless it is a run from an editor, a bisect step, a reproduction, or a partial run from a developer&#39;s machine or the desktop app (<a href="/reference/test-metadata.html#run-origin">run origin</a>):</p><ul><li><strong>A summary comment</strong> on the run&#39;s open pull request (the one its CI names when the CI runs in the repository Piwi posts to, a pull request from a fork included, else the one open on its branch): one comment per pull request, edited on each later run rather than appended, so a busy branch doesn&#39;t collect a comment per push.</li><li><strong>A commit status</strong>: passed or failed against the run&#39;s commit, pull request or not, so a pull request shows the result in its checks list. Required for a branch-protection rule. A run whose only failures are <a href="./flaky-tests.html#quarantine-with-a-way-out">quarantined</a> tests passes, and the status says how many (<code>118/120 passed, 2 quarantined</code>); turn on <strong>Quarantined failures fail the commit status</strong> under the project&#39;s <strong>Settings → Source control</strong> to fail it on any failure. <strong>Only comment on failures</strong> follows the same rule: such a run gets no comment, unless its change left a file no test reached, which the <a href="./uncovered-changes.html">uncovered changes</a> section names. When the run has a diff to read, a second status that never fails joins it, <code>/change-coverage</code> (<a href="./uncovered-changes.html#on-the-pull-request">what it counts</a>).</li></ul><p>What the comment says, in this order:</p><ol><li><strong>New failures</strong>: failing now, passing in the last green run. This change caused them.</li><li><strong>Pre-existing failures</strong>: already broken before this change. Separated so nobody debugs someone else&#39;s bug.</li><li><strong>Flaky</strong>: passed on a retry after an earlier attempt failed.</li><li><strong>Looks fixed</strong>: <code>test.fail()</code> tests that passed, each with &quot;remove <code>test.fail()</code>&quot; and its file, since the bug they reproduce no longer shows.</li><li><strong>New failure clusters</strong>: root causes never seen before in this project.</li><li><strong>Fixed by this change</strong>: clusters this pull request closed, with how long they were open. See <a href="./failure-clusters.html#did-the-fix-work">Did the fix work?</a></li><li><strong>Left open by this change</strong>: <a href="./resource-leaks.html#in-ci">resource leaks</a> the base branch never showed, each with the line that opened it.</li></ol><p>Each failure carries its error, its owner and tags when the test declares them (see <a href="/reference/test-metadata.html#ownership-metadata-piwi-annotations">ownership metadata</a>), and, when a locator broke, the <a href="./locator-healing.html">replacement locator</a> captured from the last passing run. The footer reports the CI minutes the run spent on waits and failed attempts. When there is a diff to work with, the comment adds <a href="./uncovered-changes.html">Uncovered changes</a>: the changed files no test reached and, with <a href="./combined-coverage.html">combined coverage</a>, the changed lines no suite ran, then <strong>Locators this change breaks</strong>: the locators whose label, test id, placeholder or translation the diff removes or renames, matched against the base branch&#39;s locator index as <a href="./preflight.html">Locator preflight</a> does. It lists only breaks none of whose tests ran in this run (a failing one is already listed with its failure, a passing one was updated), so it names what the run did not exercise: tests outside the selection, on another shard set, or in a nightly suite. Each comes with its call site and, for a rename, the rewritten locator.</p><figure><img src="'+c+'" alt="The summary comment on a pull request: the run&#39;s counts, one new failure with its headline, owner and suggested locator, a pre-existing failure tracked in Jira, a flaky test also flaky on main, a new cluster, a cluster this change fixed, and the CI minutes wasted"><figcaption>The summary comment on a pull request.</figcaption></figure><h2 id="the-gate-verdict" tabindex="-1">The gate verdict <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-gate-verdict" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The gate verdict&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each <a href="/guide/ci.html#blocking-a-merge">merge gate</a> evaluation made with a key that can upload or control the project&#39;s runs (the CI key <code>piwi gate</code> uses) is stored with its policy, its verdict, its violations and the pull request it judged. A reader of the project gets the verdict only: nothing is stored or posted.</p><ul><li><strong>A commit status of its own.</strong> Turn on <strong>Gate status</strong> under the project&#39;s <strong>Settings → Source control</strong> and each verdict is also posted as your status context plus <code>/gate</code> (<code>piwi/tests/gate</code>), so a branch rule can require the gate rather than the run. It needs <strong>Set a commit status</strong> on. An <a href="./environment-incidents.html">environment incident</a> posts nothing.</li><li><strong>Overrides and escapes.</strong> Every ten minutes Piwi asks the host what became of the pull requests a gate failed on. One merged while its last evaluation still failed counts as an override; one fixed first, or closed, does not. When a cluster that evaluation caught as a new regression fails again on an eligible default-branch run within 30 days of the merge, the override also counts as an escape past the gate.</li></ul><h2 id="turn-it-on" tabindex="-1">Turn it on <a class="header-anchor" href="#turn-it-on" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Turn it on&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Turn it on in <strong>Settings → Pull requests</strong> (off by default). It needs:</p><ul><li><strong><code>PIWI_SITE_URL</code></strong> set on the dashboard. Every link in the comment is built from it; without it nothing is posted, because a comment full of unreachable links is worse than no comment.</li><li><strong>An SCM token with write access</strong> to the repository, set instance-wide or per project: <code>repo</code> on GitHub, <code>api</code> on GitLab, <code>repository</code> and <code>pullrequest</code> on Bitbucket. <a href="/guide/source-control.html">Source control</a> explains where to set it and what else uses it.</li><li><strong>Branch and commit metadata on the run</strong>, which the reporter <a href="/guide/ci.html#what-gets-detected">detects automatically</a>.</li></ul><h2 id="limits" tabindex="-1">Limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Limits&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Pull-request feedback posts on GitHub, GitLab and Bitbucket Cloud, not on Bitbucket Server or Data Center. On Bitbucket the commit status is a build status keyed by the status context, shown on the pull request of the run&#39;s branch, and the comment&#39;s marker is a link reference definition, which Markdown does not render.</li><li>Piwi only ever edits a comment it wrote itself (identified by a hidden marker), so a human comment is never overwritten.</li><li>Everything here is best-effort: the run is already stored by the time it runs, and a missing token or an unreachable host is logged rather than failing your pipeline.</li><li>A comment reports; it does not block. To fail the build on a policy, use the <a href="/guide/ci.html#blocking-a-merge">merge gate</a>.</li></ul><h2 id="related" tabindex="-1">Related <a class="header-anchor" href="#related" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Related&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="/guide/ci.html">CI &amp; sharding</a>: run the reporter in CI and gate a merge</li><li><a href="/guide/source-control.html">Source control</a>: the SCM token and its scopes</li><li><a href="./auto-heal.html">Auto-heal PRs</a>: the pull request Piwi opens itself</li><li><a href="./failure-clusters.html">Failure clusters &amp; the inbox</a>: the clusters the comment reports</li><li><a href="./ci-rerun.html">Re-run in CI</a>: re-run a cluster&#39;s tests from the dashboard</li></ul>',18))])}const y=s(u,[["render",d]]);export{k as __pageData,y as default};
